#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192311 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1acdfb7b-9e3d-5475-bb07-d5e2944294f2" :knowledge-point/title "Using an Implicitly Defined Curve to Determine a Second Coordinate"}] :question/id #uuid "4b721d58-c019-5874-a194-0564792d6fe9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10900" :question/problem "Consider the curve $x^3-\\ln y=1.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=2$ on the curve at the point where $x=1,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=2$ and $x=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =1 \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192312 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "522fc628-3f81-566b-b7ce-10c75b626c0e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate From an Implicitly Defined Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "399c1168-d80e-5969-9c93-0ab34f7576a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-10912" :question/problem "Consider the following ellipse: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{4}=1\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2}$ on the curve at the point $(1,\\sqrt2),$ find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (x)+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{y}{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=\\sqrt{2},$ $x=1,$ and $y=\\sqrt2,$ and solve $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}.$  We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (1)+\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192325 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05dd2261-9fbc-5f6c-8c75-357e656b6e6d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Equation of a Conic Section to Determine a Second Coordinate"}] :question/id #uuid "18eca494-51ed-5f9c-95cc-6c9dc8efd474" :question/math-academy-id "e-10963" :question/problem "Consider the section of the hyperbola \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{3}-\\dfrac{y^2}{2}=1\n$$\n\nthat lies in the first quadrant. Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=-2$ on the hyperbola at the point where $x=3,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}$ at this point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}=-2$ and $x=3.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2\\cdot 3}{3})-(-2)\\cdot y & =0 \\\\ 2\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also want to substitute for $y$, but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=3$ into the equation of the hyperbola.   \n\nAt the point where $x=3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{3}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y > 0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $y= 2,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1acdfb7b-9e3d-5475-bb07-d5e2944294f2" :knowledge-point/title "Using an Implicitly Defined Curve to Determine a Second Coordinate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afc8d9a0-7985-584e-9749-601e1b4c9573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350832} {:answer/id #uuid "667900bd-230f-52d7-abda-9a5bd8ebd44d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350833} {:answer/id #uuid "51fd75c5-af6f-53d9-abda-7b741f728e61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350834} {:answer/id #uuid "adc56347-3f8d-57ea-9647-f6fec1831a05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350835} {:answer/id #uuid "5c433b23-67ad-5153-a553-bc8433ec2c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "667900bd-230f-52d7-abda-9a5bd8ebd44d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350833} :answer-field/id #uuid "46aa1a6e-7848-5412-abcf-a4624c993f3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e69b3edd-f7b5-5b53-8fa2-bbbd0997ab35" :question/math-academy-id "q-115951" :question/problem "Consider the curve ${e}^{x}-{e}^{y}=y.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3$ on the curve at the point where $y=0,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & =y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{x}-{e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({e}^{y}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{x}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot {e}^{x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot ({e}^{y}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3$ and $y=0.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)\\cdot {e}^{x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot ({e}^{0}+1) \\\\ -3{e}^{x} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=0$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x}-{e}^{0} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}-1 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{0} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -3 & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350837 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "522fc628-3f81-566b-b7ce-10c75b626c0e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate From an Implicitly Defined Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8445d5cf-1d54-5b55-be5b-6353049bcbd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350839} {:answer/id #uuid "0c6d940c-5925-5dd5-b082-a26e52c1940e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350840} {:answer/id #uuid "544b31d8-4e4f-564d-85cf-f814f0c1173f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350841} {:answer/id #uuid "3db99176-9c9c-57b0-9c17-09a65abd459e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350842} {:answer/id #uuid "04359439-d04f-5d28-9266-b15eba055524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8445d5cf-1d54-5b55-be5b-6353049bcbd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350839} :answer-field/id #uuid "55472616-9283-510f-9718-4007fc846ca1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6df09523-ee81-522d-8c6a-11a8d41e8c78" :question/math-academy-id "q-156756" :question/problem "Consider the curve ${e}^{x}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=1.$ Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3$ on the curve at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot ({e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3,$ $x=0,$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and solve $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {e}^{0}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) & =0 \\\\ 3+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350844 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "522fc628-3f81-566b-b7ce-10c75b626c0e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate From an Implicitly Defined Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8062d091-7971-5537-8dba-14ef4167ad05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350846} {:answer/id #uuid "f13b00c8-2606-53df-8ae0-cdc5d245f26b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350847} {:answer/id #uuid "277a71be-755b-5855-bd53-a3dfeb80dd3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350848} {:answer/id #uuid "8566a2f7-596b-5622-ad7b-aa564d023697" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350849} {:answer/id #uuid "2f2a722f-3b01-57cf-b67d-c8d113f4d31d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8062d091-7971-5537-8dba-14ef4167ad05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350846} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0231755-ae39-548b-801f-b6c7792d3592" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "206d989b-0255-5e02-9563-76f3e56023b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-156854" :question/problem "Consider the parabola ${x}^{2}-8y=0.$ Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{8}$ on the curve at the point $(4,2),$ find the value of  $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}-8y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(8y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{8},$ $x=4,$ and $y=2,$ and solve $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2\\cdot (4))-(\\frac{1}{8})\\cdot 8 & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-1 & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350851 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1acdfb7b-9e3d-5475-bb07-d5e2944294f2" :knowledge-point/title "Using an Implicitly Defined Curve to Determine a Second Coordinate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c685ad4-2d00-5d2e-8b01-107c67cfd9da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350853} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9ac095-47e4-52a5-bfd2-1c73d7d927e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350854} {:answer/id #uuid "71fb5a6b-dea5-5726-8ca9-1c9c02bd76ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350855} {:answer/id #uuid "2a0d9755-f71d-5936-a0bd-ad204fd66b26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350856} {:answer/id #uuid "234ea0e0-2dcb-55d4-86a1-bf2410f2e01f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a0d9755-f71d-5936-a0bd-ad204fd66b26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350856} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2cb001c-d935-54ba-b1bd-d1e1109d9d50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "519fa7b6-ca5f-5569-866d-fa37cd3571f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-157100" :question/problem "Consider the curve ${x}^{3}-4{y}^{2}-2y=2.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2$ on the curve at the point where $y=1,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-4{y}^{2}-2y & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}-4{y}^{2}-2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{y}^{2}+2y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4{y}^{2}+2y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (8y+2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2$ and $y=1.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (3{x}^{2})-2\\cdot 10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $y=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-4\\cdot {1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3}-6 & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =8 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 3\\cdot {2}^{2}-20 & =0 \\\\ 12\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1acdfb7b-9e3d-5475-bb07-d5e2944294f2" :knowledge-point/title "Using an Implicitly Defined Curve to Determine a Second Coordinate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "863853d1-33a7-5ab9-8dd2-0b325b8c83c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350860} {:answer/id #uuid "84ab052f-0553-5e1b-ace7-06102c756c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350861} {:answer/id #uuid "3b93d926-a16d-56a9-be4b-c7e0faed509d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350862} {:answer/id #uuid "8eca2426-4a8f-528b-abb8-e979e63d4ade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350863} {:answer/id #uuid "07bb9102-a94d-5412-9044-ca5e6f31da88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eca2426-4a8f-528b-abb8-e979e63d4ade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350863} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dab778d-eeae-5608-95ca-a17b669177c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ece7f34-4d2a-5654-a617-e42b32c6698a" :question/math-academy-id "q-157155" :question/problem "Consider the curve $-{y}^{3}+2(x-2)^{2}=10.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{4}$ on the curve at the point where $x=3,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{3}+2(x-2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{y}^{3}+2(x-2)^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2(x-2)^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(-{y}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2(x-2)^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-3{y}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4(x-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-3{y}^{2})+\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4(3-2) & =0 \\\\ (-3{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=3$ into the equation of the curve. \n\nAt the point where $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{3}+2(3-2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ -{y}^{3}+2 & =10 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =-8 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3(-2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+1 & =0 \\\\ -12\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05dd2261-9fbc-5f6c-8c75-357e656b6e6d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Equation of a Conic Section to Determine a Second Coordinate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f4df552-e65f-5f7c-b611-e6e1f5c324e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350867} {:answer/id #uuid "27a82f23-48f6-5712-b516-6175ae291fd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350868} {:answer/id #uuid "71d945ef-f139-5728-9579-6034bf452207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350869} {:answer/id #uuid "7659bfd1-415c-58ff-8ad2-a83489c1bd4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350870} {:answer/id #uuid "03cda9a5-525c-5a64-987d-43af801e30ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7659bfd1-415c-58ff-8ad2-a83489c1bd4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350870} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0124f6d-361e-5c35-b2b1-a04d8eafadbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f989e37-ce03-5456-a2b4-c69015c9335f" :question/math-academy-id "q-157156" :question/problem "Consider the section of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-{y}^{2}=1$ that lies in the first quadrant. Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6$ on the curve at the point where $x=2\\sqrt{3},$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6$ and $x=2\\sqrt{3}.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot (\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}}{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y) & =0 \\\\ 8\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=2\\sqrt{3}$ into the equation of the hyperbola.   \n\nAt the point where $x=2\\sqrt{3},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}}{3}-{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y>0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $y=\\sqrt{3},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2(\\sqrt{3}) & =0 \\\\ 8\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05dd2261-9fbc-5f6c-8c75-357e656b6e6d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Equation of a Conic Section to Determine a Second Coordinate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61aa2d0a-4a64-522d-bf51-db11cb779c89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350874} {:answer/id #uuid "be147a5e-c205-5a2b-8398-8f99ca19b89a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350875} {:answer/id #uuid "90180d0e-fd76-54c0-92e7-88981e2c9547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350876} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c952aa-5bf4-519c-b2cb-4e007c3b6469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350877} {:answer/id #uuid "22cd9eb3-2601-53db-9f5b-163e80400bef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90180d0e-fd76-54c0-92e7-88981e2c9547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350876} :answer-field/id #uuid "e37714ff-8e9d-530c-896d-d3d236b812c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0038fe2b-9ea2-568d-8c5f-5ccff66bc5ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-157217" :question/problem "Consider the section of the parabola $3{x}^{2}+4y=1$ that lies in the fourth quadrant.  Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{4}$ on the parabola at the point where $y=-\\frac{1}{2},$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{x}^{2}+4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6x)+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (6x)+(-\\frac{1}{4})\\cdot (4) & =0 \\\\ 6x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=-\\frac{1}{2}$ into the equation of  the parabola.  \n\nAt the point where $y=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+4y & =1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) & =1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2 & =1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $x>0$ since we're only interested in the fourth quadrant.  \n\nFinally, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot (1)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-1 & =0 \\\\ 6\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05dd2261-9fbc-5f6c-8c75-357e656b6e6d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Equation of a Conic Section to Determine a Second Coordinate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5742eaa-9265-569a-856e-0bee7b777a65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350881} {:answer/id #uuid "ac43df3e-7b9e-5b06-b195-3d4dc9809d2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350882} {:answer/id #uuid "ae78514a-453a-50e2-b5a9-ba7af0655a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350883} {:answer/id #uuid "306d6997-f9d1-592d-9001-8763a8900604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350884} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6b9d2e-bfd2-5900-989c-86bf05de01f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "306d6997-f9d1-592d-9001-8763a8900604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350884} :answer-field/id #uuid "62cadd3a-5feb-54f5-886d-7e123439af89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7943571e-e0d7-5d55-aed7-57f940c9ba1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-157223" :question/problem "Consider the section of the parabola ${x}^{2}-4y=4$ that lies in the first quadrant.  Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3$ on the parabola at the point where $y=3,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4y & =4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}-4y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3$ and $y=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2x)-(3)\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ 2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=3$ into the equation of  the parabola.  \n\nAt the point where $y=3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4y & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4\\cdot (3) & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-12 & =4 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $x>0$ since we're only interested in the  first  quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-12 & =0 \\\\ 8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350886 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05dd2261-9fbc-5f6c-8c75-357e656b6e6d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Equation of a Conic Section to Determine a Second Coordinate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91517c83-2ea3-5220-9dde-4ca19bcb1bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91517c83-2ea3-5220-9dde-4ca19bcb1bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350888} :answer-field/id #uuid "07da92f5-1ddd-5fd4-8459-05fa6d6e0e6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2236dd12-34e6-53be-8844-5c2e0ccb86ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-313938" :question/problem "Consider the section of the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1$ that lies in the second quadrant. Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=12$ on the hyperbola at the point where $x=-5,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{9})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2y}{9}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=12$ and $x=-5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\cdot (\\frac{2(-5)}{9})-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2y}{9}) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{120}{9}-\\frac{2y}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=-5$ into the equation of the hyperbola.\n\nAt the point where $x=-5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(-5)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{25}{9}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 25-{y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $y>0$ since we're only interested in the second quadrant.\n\nFinally, substituting $y=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{120}{9}-\\frac{2(4)}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{120}{9}-\\frac{8}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{8}{9}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{120}{9} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{120}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1acdfb7b-9e3d-5475-bb07-d5e2944294f2" :knowledge-point/title "Using an Implicitly Defined Curve to Determine a Second Coordinate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "489a1c30-b881-5af4-b75c-cebd5f0538ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "489a1c30-b881-5af4-b75c-cebd5f0538ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350891} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e932c59-180a-5f69-865e-cf93e19d919e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7e8c2e6-523f-56e4-8b8c-66cf999bb02e" :question/math-academy-id "q-315191" :question/problem "Consider the curve ${y}^{2}+2y=-x.$ Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2$ on the curve at the point where $x=1,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y & =-x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2}+2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2}+2y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y+2) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y+2) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2$ and $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2y+2) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=1$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2y & =-x \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y & =-1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2y+1 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2y+2) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(2\\cdot (-1)+2) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(0) & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "522fc628-3f81-566b-b7ce-10c75b626c0e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate From an Implicitly Defined Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5949430-27b3-53fb-8a6c-2e394adc535e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5949430-27b3-53fb-8a6c-2e394adc535e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350894} :answer-field/id #uuid "81eb7b30-0e4b-5a53-9b7d-95ede5b95068" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ccdec465-41c1-53e9-9934-e5b569cf900d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343557" :question/problem "Consider the parabola ${y}^{2}-20x=0.$ Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{10}$ on the curve at the point $(5,10),$ find the value of  $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-20x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2}-20x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(20x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(20x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 20 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{10},$ $x=5,$ and $y=10,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2y)-\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 20 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (2\\cdot 10)-(\\frac{3}{10})\\cdot 20 & =0 \\\\ 20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-6 & =0 \\\\ 20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350895 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "522fc628-3f81-566b-b7ce-10c75b626c0e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate From an Implicitly Defined Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a354e20c-52b5-5136-a1f3-b1815fb8f4f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a354e20c-52b5-5136-a1f3-b1815fb8f4f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350897} :answer-field/id #uuid "c25a18dd-24ec-5fac-8919-e9f60ad64fa3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "474ec9e7-52c1-5cfb-9fbe-0b75a25440dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343738" :question/problem "$$\n\nConsider the curve $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=2.$ Given that $x=x(t),\\text{ }y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6$ on the curve at the point $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6,$ $x=4,$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 6\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 3-2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350898 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "522fc628-3f81-566b-b7ce-10c75b626c0e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate From an Implicitly Defined Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "651a33da-472b-5998-a25d-caef0ae15022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "651a33da-472b-5998-a25d-caef0ae15022" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350900} :answer-field/id #uuid "da93192d-fa1a-5cab-94fb-972b2985fd3f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9e3095a-b964-5d66-9d9d-96a578e1a670" :question/math-academy-id "q-343745" :question/problem "$$\n\nConsider the curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y=0.$ Given that $x=x(t),\\text{ }y=y(t),$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=5$ on the curve at the point $(1,0),$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=5,$ $x=1,$ and $y=0,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 5\\cdot \\frac{1}{1}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ 5+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05dd2261-9fbc-5f6c-8c75-357e656b6e6d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Equation of a Conic Section to Determine a Second Coordinate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87214a7c-d1a1-5776-9383-d77e90fa3915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350903} {:answer/id #uuid "db16fb51-b7ba-56b4-acb2-c24032479eb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87214a7c-d1a1-5776-9383-d77e90fa3915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350903} :answer-field/id #uuid "38878df5-2dfd-587d-93e5-6898cfd04772" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca10406f-7159-5346-a4b9-45702956e5e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-344033" :question/problem "Consider the section of the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1$ that lies in the first quadrant.  Given that $x=x(t),$ $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3$ on the ellipse at the point where $y=3,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point.\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{16})+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2y}{25}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, substituting the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3$ and $y=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot (\\frac{2x}{16})+3\\cdot (\\frac{2(3)}{25}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{18}{25} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $x,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $y=3$ into the equation of the ellipse.  \n\nAt the point where $y=3,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{9}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{16}{25} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{256}{25} \\\\ x & =\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used $x>0$ since we're only interested in the first quadrant. \n\nFinally, substituting $x=\\frac{16}{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{18}{25} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{16}{5})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{18}{25} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{18}{25} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{18}{25}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1acdfb7b-9e3d-5475-bb07-d5e2944294f2" :knowledge-point/title "Using an Implicitly Defined Curve to Determine a Second Coordinate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c20eee6-79ed-5858-8156-3d478e04843e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191761} {:answer/id #uuid "d31c039a-7bae-5253-a877-80100e0fd2e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191762} {:answer/id #uuid "17130fe0-8dae-57cf-a1b3-9b1f39793574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191763} {:answer/id #uuid "49b028a8-5c21-5b98-a1e2-43829eadcadd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191764} {:answer/id #uuid "525fc477-40fd-55ef-ad0e-0451addadaac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17130fe0-8dae-57cf-a1b3-9b1f39793574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191763} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e5c3519-9f4c-5247-8566-9945fcff7ecd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186191760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a72cc8b-f0dc-5848-bf1a-1efa5e385ea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-48800" :question/problem "Consider the curve $x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=4.$ Given that $x=x(t),$   $y=y(t),$  and that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-1$ on the curve at the point where $x=4,$ find the value of $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ at that point." :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-1$ and $x=4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also want to substitute for $y,$ but we are not given this value. However, we can find it by substituting $x=4$ into the equation of the curve.\n\nAt the point where $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4 \\\\ 4+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
